WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.578
This paper, presented at ICML 2024,

00:00:02.658 --> 00:00:08.249
tackles one-shot imitation learning when the world
changes after the demonstration is given.

00:00:08.329 --> 00:00:11.763
The authors introduce Deep Demonstration Tracing, or DDT,

00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:17.681
a method that lets an imitator agent adaptively
trace the right states in a single demonstration

00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:20.331
while recovering from unforeseen obstacles.

00:00:20.411 --> 00:00:26.866
DDT combines a purpose-built demonstration
transformer with a meta-reinforcement-learning training scheme,

00:00:26.946 --> 00:00:34.140
and it outperforms prior one-shot imitation methods
across a new maze navigation benchmark and several robotics tasks.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:40.721
One-shot imitation learning asks an agent to carry
out a task after seeing just a single demonstration.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:44.319
It works well when deployment looks like the
demonstration,

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:46.148
but the real world is dynamic.

00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:50.478
After the demonstration is provided, an unexpected
obstacle can appear,

00:00:50.558 --> 00:00:52.246
or a grasped object can slip,

00:00:52.326 --> 00:00:56.271
pushing the agent into situations the demonstration
never covered.

00:00:56.351 --> 00:01:00.417
Traditional one-shot imitation methods excel in
stationary settings,

00:01:00.497 --> 00:01:05.478
yet their ability to handle these unforeseen
changes is limited and rarely studied.

00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:12.430
This paper focuses squarely on that gap: making
one-shot imitation robust when the environment changes at runtime.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.714
Consider a person following a demonstrated route
from a start point to a destination.

00:01:17.794 --> 00:01:21.585
Partway there, a truck is parked where the
demonstration had none.

00:01:21.665 --> 00:01:26.862
A human simply detours around it and then rejoins
the original path at a convenient point.

00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:31.261
This is easy for people but hard for current
one-shot imitation techniques,

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:38.180
which mostly clone demonstrated actions and have no
principled way to behave in states the demonstration never showed.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.578
The authors distill this human behavior into a
three-stage decision process

00:01:42.658 --> 00:01:45.393
identify which demonstrated states are relevant,

00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:49.791
analyze how the expert behaved there, and trace
back onto the demonstration

00:01:49.871 --> 00:01:52.430
and use it as the blueprint for their method.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.260
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.790
First,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.320
it advances the one-shot imitation setting by
deliberately introducing a large difference

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.850
between when the demonstration is collected and
when the policy is deployed,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.380
and it supports this with a new
demonstration-navigation benchmark.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.910
Second,

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:17.440
it proposes a demonstration transformer
architecture that encourages the policy to trace the demonstration,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.970
following the three-stage identify, analyze, and
trace process.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.500
Third,

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.030
it addresses one-shot imitation as a context-based
meta-reinforcement-learning problem,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.560
and it theoretically analyzes the conditions under
which an imitator can succeed from just a single trajectory.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.322
DDT has two core pieces.

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:38.784
The first is the demonstration transformer,

00:02:38.864 --> 00:02:42.246
which turns the human three-stage process into a network.

00:02:42.326 --> 00:02:45.707
The agent's current state becomes a query;

00:02:45.787 --> 00:02:49.169
the demonstration's states and actions become keys
and values.

00:02:49.249 --> 00:02:52.631
An attention-weighting module identifies which
demonstrated states to follow,

00:02:52.711 --> 00:02:56.093
a point-wise multiplication analyzes how the expert
behaved in those states,

00:02:56.173 --> 00:02:59.555
and the result is combined with the current state
to produce the action

00:02:59.635 --> 00:03:03.017
the tracing step.

00:03:03.097 --> 00:03:06.479
The second piece is training.

00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:09.941
Rather than behavior cloning,

00:03:10.021 --> 00:03:13.402
DDT frames one-shot imitation as context-based
meta-reinforcement learning,

00:03:13.482 --> 00:03:16.864
so the agent can explore and learn to act in states
the demonstration never visited.

00:03:16.944 --> 00:03:20.326
It uses a carefully designed stationary OSIL reward
that rewards following the demonstration

00:03:20.406 --> 00:03:23.788
while a large-weighted task reward keeps the focus
on actually completing the task,

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:27.250
and it optimizes everything with Soft Actor-Critic.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.209
To test one-shot imitation under unforeseen change,

00:03:31.289 --> 00:03:34.867
the authors created a benchmark called Valet
Parking Assist in Maze, or VPAM,

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:38.526
inspired by real-world valet parking.

00:03:38.606 --> 00:03:42.184
In it, a point agent must reach a target in a maze
it has never seen globally,

00:03:42.264 --> 00:03:45.843
relying only on short local views computed from
eight rays.

00:03:45.923 --> 00:03:49.501
Crucially,

00:03:49.581 --> 00:03:53.160
rectangular obstacles are randomly placed on the
path and often did not exist in the demonstration,

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.819
so the agent cannot blindly replay demonstrated actions.

00:03:56.899 --> 00:04:00.477
Eight task variants change whether it is a single
map or many,

00:04:00.557 --> 00:04:04.136
whether obstacles are present, and whether
coordinates are given.

00:04:04.216 --> 00:04:07.794
Beyond VPAM,

00:04:07.874 --> 00:04:11.453
DDT is also applied to robotics benchmarks
including Meta-World with added disturbances,

00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:15.111
Gymnasium's Reacher and Pusher, and a MuJoCo
manipulation task involving grasping,

00:04:15.191 --> 00:04:18.770
stacking, and cluttered environments.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.577
Across the VPAM benchmark, DDT consistently leads.

00:04:22.657 --> 00:04:26.364
On the training set it reaches a success rate of 0.86,

00:04:26.444 --> 00:04:31.695
on non-obstacle test settings 0.84, and even under
unforeseen obstacles 0.73

00:04:31.775 --> 00:04:33.519
well above every baseline,

00:04:33.599 --> 00:04:40.813
with the closest competitor DCRL at 0.57 under
obstacles and behavior-cloning Trans4OSIL as low as 0.16.

00:04:40.893 --> 00:04:47.196
Just as important is stability: moving from the
training condition to unforeseen obstacles,

00:04:47.276 --> 00:04:52.667
DDT's performance drops only fifteen percent, while
the baselines drop twenty,

00:04:52.747 --> 00:04:56.104
thirty-three, and fifty-two percent respectively.

00:04:56.184 --> 00:05:00.031
On the Meta-World robotics tasks with added disturbance,

00:05:00.111 --> 00:05:05.222
DDT still succeeds sixty-one percent of the time on
unseen demonstrations,

00:05:05.302 --> 00:05:09.781
whereas the strongest baseline manages only about
twelve percent.

00:05:09.861 --> 00:05:14.410
The meta-RL mechanism is what gives DDT this
robustness to change.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:18.658
To confirm each component matters, the authors
ablate two pieces.

00:05:18.738 --> 00:05:23.153
First, they swap the demonstration transformer for
a standard transformer;

00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:26.798
this significantly reduces the final asymptotic
performance,

00:05:26.878 --> 00:05:31.354
underscoring that the tailored architecture, not
just attention in general,

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:33.723
is what drives DDT's imitation ability.

00:05:33.803 --> 00:05:38.643
Second, they remove the OSIL reward and train with
only the sparse ending reward;

00:05:38.723 --> 00:05:42.895
this sharply slows learning, because the OSIL
reward supplies a dense,

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:48.362
informative signal that lets the agent closely
follow the demonstration early in training.

00:05:48.442 --> 00:05:55.530
Together the ablations show the demonstration
transformer and the OSIL reward each play a distinct and necessary role.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.472
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:59.552 --> 00:06:03.114
Under unforeseen obstacles, DDT succeeds
seventy-three percent of the time,

00:06:03.194 --> 00:06:06.755
compared to fifty-seven percent for the best baseline.

00:06:06.835 --> 00:06:10.397
Its performance degradation from training to
unforeseen obstacles is just fifteen percent,

00:06:10.477 --> 00:06:14.039
at least five percent better retention than any
competitor.

00:06:14.119 --> 00:06:17.681
On disturbed Meta-World tasks it reaches sixty-one
percent on unseen demonstrations while

00:06:17.761 --> 00:06:21.323
baselines stay at or below twelve percent.

00:06:21.403 --> 00:06:24.965
And when scaled up,

00:06:25.045 --> 00:06:28.606
DDT improves roughly two-fold with more model
parameters and shows a clean log-linear

00:06:28.686 --> 00:06:32.248
gain with both data volume and model size,

00:06:32.328 --> 00:06:35.890
hinting at its promise as a backbone for generalist
agents.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.543
The takeaway is simple.

00:06:37.623 --> 00:06:42.192
Instead of blindly replaying a demonstration, an
agent should learn to trace it

00:06:42.272 --> 00:06:45.841
figuring out which demonstrated states are relevant
right now,

00:06:45.921 --> 00:06:48.195
understanding what the expert did there,

00:06:48.275 --> 00:06:50.960
and steering back onto the path after a detour.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:56.139
Deep Demonstration Tracing operationalizes this
with a demonstration transformer trained

00:06:56.219 --> 00:06:57.963
by meta-reinforcement learning,

00:06:58.043 --> 00:07:03.671
and the result is one-shot imitation that stays
robust when the environment changes unexpectedly,

00:07:03.751 --> 00:07:05.790
a regime where earlier methods fail.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.792
Its clean scaling behavior even suggests it could
become a building block for larger,

00:07:10.872 --> 00:07:12.910
more general decision-making agents.
